I may be able to supply a partial answer.

I suppose it depends on your play style, but in my limited experience,
Tourists, being neither extremely buff physically nor primary casters,
have a tendency to rely somewhat on certain kinds of magic items --
wands, potions, even rings: things Thor's hammer destroys.

It's a trade-off, of course, and I tend to like the Tourist's trade-
off,
if I manage to survive to mid-game, because you don't get quite the
worst of either end of the bargain:  you are neither as physically
wimpy nor have as many weapon restrictions as primary casters
typically do, but you can cast spells in most schools up to level
three, maybe higher with a robe and no metal armor, which is
enough that you don't have to rely on a carefully managed
inventory of scrolls to the same extent as barbarians and such.
You can cast identify, for example, and remove curse.

Nonetheless, there are things you do need.  Unlike a Valkyrie,
you can't just whack everything with +3 Excalibur and kill it in
one or two hits, and unlike a Wizard you can't usefully cast
high-level attack spells.  Unlike a Barbarian, you can't let
yourself get surrounded by high-level monsters, even of the
non-nasty variety, for very long without sustaining quite
noticeable amounts of damage.  So you tend to want to always
carry stuff like a ring of conflict, a ring of regeneration, wands
of cold and fire and lightning, and so on.

I don't know for sure if that's what the other poster is thinking,
but having had one of my three longest games so far be a
Tourist, this is some of my reasoning for avoiding weapons
that destroy magic items.

Then again, I tend to avoid them playing other roles too,
so maybe it's just that "waste not want not" that got drilled
into me as a child.  (We were in the economic bracket where
gardening was an important aspect of balancing the budget,
so we were not allowed to, for example, throw away some
of the food on our plate when we decided we weren't hungry
any more.  I'm not as extreme as my mom's parents, who
were married during the depression and raised frugality to
a high art, but perhaps a limited amount of thrift did rub off
on me.)


